`npm test` passes: 3 files, 5/5 tests.

The requested coverage is in `tests/resize-observer.test.ts`:

```ts
test('destroy disconnects the ResizeObserver', () => {
  ...
  const cg = Chessground(wrap, { viewOnly: true });
  expect(observers).toHaveLength(1);        // one RO, observing the wrap
  cg.destroy();
  expect(live()).toHaveLength(0);           // disconnected on destroy
});

test('redrawAll disconnects previous ResizeObservers', () => {
  ...
  cg.redrawAll();
  cg.redrawAll();
  expect(observers).toHaveLength(3);        // 3 constructed total
  expect(live()).toHaveLength(1);           // no duplication: only 1 live
  expect(live()[0].observed).toEqual([cg.state.dom.elements.wrap]); // the current wrap
});
```

Mechanics: a mock `ResizeObserver` is injected via `vi.stubGlobal` and records each instance plus `disconnect()` calls. jsdom ships no native RO and chessground checks `'ResizeObserver' in window` at bind time, so the production code path in `bindBoard` runs against the mock.

Proof of efficacy: both tests were verified red on the unfixed code (1 live observer surviving `destroy()`; 3 live observers after 3 renders) and green after the fix — so they fail if the leak regresses in either form.

